 \section{Related Literature}
 
-Three strands of work frame the study. The first concerns the magnitude of completeness. A catalogue is complete above $M_c$ if it contains all events above that magnitude, and a frequency--magnitude distribution estimated below $M_c$ is flattened by missing small events, which lowers the estimated $b$. Wiemer and Wyss (\citeyear{wiemer2000minimum}) examined how to estimate the minimum magnitude of completeness from the frequency--magnitude distribution and showed that it varies in space and time; Woessner and Wiemer (\citeyear{woessner2005assessing}) quantified the uncertainty of such estimates; more recent comparisons of catalogue-based completeness methods \citep{wang2025estimating} and proposals to derive an incompleteness magnitude from an unbiased estimate of $b$ \citep{godano2023evaluating} indicate that the choice of rule remains consequential. We use two of the established rules, maximum curvature with a correction and a goodness-of-fit criterion, and we report how far results move when the rule or the threshold changes. Our study differs in that it applies them decade by decade to a single global catalogue whose magnitude scale itself changes.
-
-The second strand concerns the estimation and precision of the $b$-value. The standard maximum-likelihood estimator of the $b$-value, due to Aki and Utsu, uses the mean magnitude above $M_c$ with a correction for magnitudes reported in bins; \citet{shi1982the} derived a standard error that accounts for the dispersion of magnitudes and differs from the simpler $b/\sqrt{N}$ attributed to Aki. The precision of the estimate depends on the number of events \citep{kamer2014minimum}, which is large in every decade of our sample; the practical limit is therefore the set of assumptions behind the standard error, chiefly that events are independent and the sample homogeneous. We take up this point in the discussion.
-
