 \section{Discussion}
 
-The yearly number of events of magnitude 5 and larger in ComCat has no persistent direction over 1976--2025. The catalogue records a mean of about 1,670 events per year, a lower decade in 1996--2005 and a higher decade in 2006--2015, and the counts at magnitudes 6 and 7 do not share this shape. The popular statement that earthquakes are increasing is therefore not supported by decade means of the counts at magnitude 5 and larger, although the small positive log-linear slope and the high decade of 2006--2015 show where such a statement could come from. These years contain the aftershock sequences of the largest events, and the preferred magnitude type changes from body-wave to moment magnitudes within the same window.
-
-The $b$-value is not stable across decades in the full sample once $M_c$ is estimated per decade, and the instability survives a common threshold, a fixed threshold, and the restriction to shallow events. It does not survive in the same form in the moment-magnitude sample. Two readings are open to us. A physical change in the size distribution would affect every magnitude type, yet it is absent from the moment-magnitude sample at its own, higher completeness magnitude; a change in how magnitudes are determined for events between 4.7 and 5.2 would affect the full sample and not the moment-magnitude sample. The moment-magnitude sample has a completeness magnitude of 5.2 to 5.6 and far fewer events, so the contrast cannot separate the two readings; both remain open, and we do not claim either. What the analysis does establish is that a $b$-value estimated from ComCat in one decade cannot be transferred to another decade without reference to the completeness threshold and the magnitude types involved.
